672d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392589}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d2d358ee-4f00-5039-8abe-a47808bd6f20" :question/math-academy-id "q-47669" :question/problem "Which of the following functions is not differentiable over the entire interval $x\\in (-1,1)$ due to the existence of a vertical tangent in the interval?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the function $y=h(x)$ has a vertical tangent at $x=0$ and is therefore not differentiable at $x=0.$ \nTherefore, the correct answer is $y=h(x).$\n\nThe other graphs do not have a vertical tangent inside the interval $(-1,1).$\n\n- The function $y=f(x)$ has a corner point at $x=0.$\n\n- The function $y=g(x)$ is smooth and continuous over the entire interval $x\\in (-1,1).$\n\n- The functions $y=p(x)$ and $y=q(x)$ are not defined at $x=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329825 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115382 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dcbd56f-0fc2-59c5-a016-4272be4734c7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points at Which a Function is Not Differentiable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cf7ce1a-6058-5910-9e37-9c52f5803f51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327752} {:answer/id #uuid "83d1af3e-3afd-5d91-88f4-3ed406eb55ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328073} {:answer/id #uuid "b5bc5f0c-c5c4-5da0-8587-7d4be22ee1a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328514} {:answer/id #uuid "e7fe587a-30bf-504d-8f35-c395e902bdb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and  III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328961} {:answer/id #uuid "f1ae9dbf-d366-51c4-8592-d07406f566fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329041}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7fe587a-30bf-504d-8f35-c395e902bdb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and  III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328961} :answer-field/id #uuid "06aff75e-c521-5c5d-91ff-ff642e60a2e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329196}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f90e0f73-a650-56a2-851e-2b406e202da6" :question/math-academy-id "q-47679" :question/problem "Given that that a function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$, has a jump discontinuity at $x=2$, and  is differentiable at $x=3$, which of the following statements must be true for **any** $f(x)$ that satisfy these criteria?\n\n- It is differentiable at $x=1$\n- It  is **not** differentiable at $x=2$\n- It is continuous at $x=3$" :question/worked-solution "- Statement I is false. Continuity does not imply differentiability. For example, the function $y=|x-1|$ is continuous but it's not differentiable at $x=1.$\n\n- Statement II is true.  A function that is discontinuous at a point can't be differentiable at this point (because differentiability implies continuity).\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, differentiability implies continuity."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329826 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115381 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b4100f8-8a66-5243-b0b6-c36b9d17dac3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Which Functions are Differentiable at a Given Point From the Function Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20ac00fa-c573-50b2-9181-c5c47c436adb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327214} {:answer/id #uuid "6664d395-2cd4-57fa-8267-dad7b7f6653c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327822} {:answer/id #uuid "ad7699cf-1310-5e2c-8483-50775b4a0f7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328441} {:answer/id #uuid "c8d74407-8722-5f57-a238-174a8da68bde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328700} {:answer/id #uuid "cf91c4ec-315b-5bb2-ba9b-e695b2ead9ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328760}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6664d395-2cd4-57fa-8267-dad7b7f6653c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327822} :answer-field/id #uuid "01e41401-65d6-5b8b-a09b-c7db46fa5956" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329187}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8298d153-fa1d-58c6-bfcd-efab6041f9f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-47681" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function given below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+1, & x\\ne 1 \\\\ 3, & x=1\\end{aligned}\\,\n$$\n\n- It is continuous and differentiable at $x=1$\n- It is **not** differentiable at $x=1$\n- It is differentiable on the interval $(-1,2)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement individually. \n\n- Statement I is false.  At $x=1$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}({x}^{3}+1)={1}^{3}+1=2 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}({x}^{3}+1)={1}^{3}+1=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne f(1)=3,\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1$ ($f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=1$).\n\n- Statement II is true. Since  $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1,$ it is also not differentiable there (because differentiability implies continuity).\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=1,$ it is not differentiable on any interval containing this point. Consequently, it is not differentiable on $(-1,2).$\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329829 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176096 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e21f194e-fb5d-5c90-af38-18d8c75582a9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Moment in Time When a Particle is Stationary"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5525d2c5-8da5-5b7a-9dec-548bb1f64862" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=5\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327670} {:answer/id #uuid "d32113f6-8db1-51c9-b343-962d9eecf5fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328787} {:answer/id #uuid "db68c9ef-ce55-599c-bc00-27824736bb7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=10\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328850} {:answer/id #uuid "dfd7c213-368f-5fb0-8c96-c6383043d8b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=8\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328895} {:answer/id #uuid "e7294816-d07d-53cb-8486-58993c4119bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=7\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5525d2c5-8da5-5b7a-9dec-548bb1f64862" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=5\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327670} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe19b9b9-08c6-5cb6-ad38-8ea58562c3f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329626}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a0beb9b2-97ad-5a80-853a-12a09696e0f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-4771" :question/problem "The position $x(t)$ of a particle (in meters) at time $t$ seconds is given by \n\n$$\nx(t)={t}^{2}-10t-2,\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the time at which the particle is stationary." :question/worked-solution "First, we need to calculate the velocity function. We can do that by taking the derivative of the position function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-10t-2) \\\\  & =2t-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m/s}.$ So, we set $v=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2t-10 & =0 \\\\ 2t & =10 \\\\ t & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at $t=5\\,\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176496 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175903 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bbc6a2d-2078-50d5-9584-c705c19212da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals on Which a Given Function Satisfies the Extreme Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20a6c5f1-fdda-51a7-9af5-55a7988c6ab3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327213} {:answer/id #uuid "2c7bf23a-3b51-50b3-b4e9-f2d073f69b2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327325} {:answer/id #uuid "2fc51e81-041a-5be8-90cd-546051875d1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327361} {:answer/id #uuid "574a128a-4326-5a1b-8f72-cb7781cce5a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327693} {:answer/id #uuid "d933b8b3-9c7e-58ef-882a-0b78b4a59d86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328832}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2fc51e81-041a-5be8-90cd-546051875d1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327361} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d6ba5c8-31d8-5c13-87be-35c5a1f633f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329303}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ee96606-8ba8-569e-8f90-21918ad24c63" :question/math-academy-id "q-47787" :question/problem "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y={log}_{2}⁡x?$ \n\n- $(0,5]$\n- $[1,5]$\n- $[0,5]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $(0,5]$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[1,5]$ is closed, and the function is continuous on $[1,5].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\n- The interval $[0,5]$ is closed, but the function is not continuous on $[0,5]$ because this interval contains points where the function is not defined. In particular, it is not defined at $x=0.$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329835 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119954 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "744b0e69-71f9-5ca0-a200-8968a9ec192e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Vertical Asymptotes of a Rational Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29a0fc8a-86ac-5ab6-acd1-3ec0072b7256" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327288} {:answer/id #uuid "b4bd35f1-1cf5-5986-8bcd-cae0e1e256a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328506} {:answer/id #uuid "e6a685ba-076b-591a-b200-e9ee6e74f0b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328951} {:answer/id #uuid "f1f4c678-2ef4-5109-bc2c-e164d61c301e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329047} {:answer/id #uuid "f598a74d-ecb1-5d47-9ac6-e6464cddea05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no vertical asymptotes." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329083}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4bd35f1-1cf5-5986-8bcd-cae0e1e256a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328506} :answer-field/id #uuid "5859fa75-27d1-5a4e-9275-4eb9c181d62a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e2ba45bb-fcdc-536e-a19c-0143c96613a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-47854" :question/problem "Find the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x)=\\frac{5}{x-\\sqrt{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "There are no common factors in the numerator and denominator. So, we find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx-\\sqrt{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $x=\\sqrt{2}$ is the only vertical asymptote of $y=f(x),$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/09/09c19a6049d77a2b57c093ca714627a453c99bf52af27ae656ad1f4e779d1866.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329837 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078428 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d752aac-6187-595d-8818-b0179fd7380e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Definite Integral Using Multiple Properties of Definite Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07c7c09f-3771-5ac2-8492-cb564408d161" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326995} {:answer/id #uuid "09a6f667-741d-5bd1-8b8a-e0169fa9064e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327016} {:answer/id #uuid "5b54c3bd-e1c0-5f6e-949c-a3321c243ed7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327735} {:answer/id #uuid "b402004a-0bc7-5a2a-89cb-61969dcaee78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328497} {:answer/id #uuid "b930f089-e00a-5693-9851-95f660218c94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328551}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b930f089-e00a-5693-9851-95f660218c94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328551} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5a34845-2a7f-510c-bb16-9154a49b42a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329468}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "99d67546-ee63-5a73-9d7b-983a73ffa720" :question/math-academy-id "q-4790" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a function such that ${\\int }_{3}^{0}\\frac{1}{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-1$ and ${\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=6.$ What is the value of ${\\int }_{0}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's simplify one of the given integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{0}\\frac{1}{3}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{3}^{0}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-1 \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, from the adjacent intervals rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3+6 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402902 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064162 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a595bc07-ad64-537d-be42-06144814b97d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Extrema of a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6390a69-73ca-5747-ad35-6ad1cc3e50ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402904} {:answer/id #uuid "bf810803-9256-56b1-b55f-ffc26fa5e427" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402905} {:answer/id #uuid "6f3e8609-ca99-5684-877b-a0d89799e552" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402906} {:answer/id #uuid "b6c87f0b-5047-5a28-9f98-5daf036d20f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402907} {:answer/id #uuid "9ca02ff7-1af5-56d8-b08a-9a759e9da705" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402908}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf810803-9256-56b1-b55f-ffc26fa5e427" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402905} :answer-field/id #uuid "e587e32b-f21e-545a-8ef6-e7a910ea785c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402903}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "126dc5db-7aa0-5e31-885e-35517971a655" :question/math-academy-id "q-47918" :question/problem "![](images/4a/4a1843255ea718116bd2daa07f1375a3fb277c47ec8503fec90da824cd714d0b.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is plotted above. Given that $f(x)$ is defined on the interval $x\\in [-2,10],$ which points represent the global maximum of $f(x)$ ?" :question/worked-solution "The global extrema of a function are the lowest or highest points of the function's graph over its entire domain.\n\nThe lowest point is the global minimum, whereas the highest point is the global maximum.\n\nAccording to the graph, the highest point is $D,$ and so this point represents the global maximum of $f(x).$ \n\n**Note:** The point $B$ is not a global maximum, it's a local maximum."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402909 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064162 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a595bc07-ad64-537d-be42-06144814b97d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Extrema of a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5af784ce-3cef-5945-8932-b03fe43316e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402911} {:answer/id #uuid "9673d400-5f29-5ce5-b5e9-8047de8eb008" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402912} {:answer/id #uuid "eb5c6830-b8b8-54b9-9b05-279cc0d24408" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402913} {:answer/id #uuid "f088b713-a43a-50e7-9574-07ac077ab3d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402914} {:answer/id #uuid "62bbee0f-e18c-5110-a2d4-144a718f657e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402915}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9673d400-5f29-5ce5-b5e9-8047de8eb008" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402912} :answer-field/id #uuid "01f254e3-3027-52e3-8cb7-edd707cacbbe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "62b39803-cf8b-5f2a-91f9-b83f48f12d0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-47919" :question/problem "![](images/d9/d9d8fb6d663d8dfd0cce24ae3817c936e2e97ab2df7aefeaa0c734caf0311b94.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is plotted above. Given that $f(x)$ is defined on the interval $x\\in [-3,10]$, which points represent the global maximum of $f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "The global extrema of a function are the lowest or highest points of the function's graph over its entire domain.\n\nThe lowest point is the global minimum, whereas the highest point is the global maximum.\n\nAccording to the graph, the highest points are $B$ and $D$, and so these points represents the global maximum of $f(x).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329841 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096621 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c547746-49c8-5a4a-85fe-c6b75493a354" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient Containing Other Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24cdb15d-92a4-57bb-b3ae-70ee6398efed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}sin⁡x-cos⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327252} {:answer/id #uuid "28cffc72-f500-56eb-b62d-297789e3d352" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}(cos⁡x+sin⁡x)}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327280} {:answer/id #uuid "537f4f22-dc76-5ecc-941c-28735a7fbb3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}sin⁡x-cos⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327658} {:answer/id #uuid "6cb04a5b-8198-547f-9f85-6d03684ff103" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}(sin⁡x-cos⁡x)}{cos⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327873} {:answer/id #uuid "6ebf154b-78ed-5323-9bc6-5af4e4ce0e17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}(sin⁡x-cos⁡x)}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28cffc72-f500-56eb-b62d-297789e3d352" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}(cos⁡x+sin⁡x)}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327280} :answer-field/id #uuid "1139e71c-df1c-5f00-8bad-e71886ea7ae5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329215}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5ace0fb1-1a71-5cfd-952c-d11857b972c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-47943" :question/problem "If $f(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{cos⁡x},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)={e}^{x}$ and $v(x)=cos⁡x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({e}^{x})^{′}\\cdot cos⁡x-{e}^{x}\\cdot (cos⁡x)^{′}}{(cos⁡x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}cos⁡x+{e}^{x}sin⁡x}{(cos⁡x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}(cos⁡x+sin⁡x)}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078970 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078428 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d752aac-6187-595d-8818-b0179fd7380e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Definite Integral Using Multiple Properties of Definite Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "355f05a8-1b69-5fc8-be47-894b11d7bfab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327397} {:answer/id #uuid "85fe28f7-f30d-502c-86da-651e061a7de4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328090} {:answer/id #uuid "aea5ab3d-2e57-5f46-9645-4bae9723adb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328450} {:answer/id #uuid "c56f6ed4-9bb4-5cc5-b67c-a8b61a3ad0c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328663} {:answer/id #uuid "e88d667c-7ec1-53b1-becf-5cea6607291d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328968}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e88d667c-7ec1-53b1-becf-5cea6607291d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328968} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e646dc1-b4b3-54e3-afbb-f460a293900c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329205}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "77fb0fc1-711e-5505-92d1-e385391bf5f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-4798" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a  function such that ${\\int }_{6}^{3}\\frac{1}{5}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-1$ and ${\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=3.$ What is the value of ${\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's simplify one of the given integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{6}^{3}\\frac{1}{5}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{6}^{3}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-1 \\\\ {\\int }_{3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, from the adjacent intervals rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3+5 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329845 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059676 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "711f198b-26b4-5185-812a-102329d49313" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of an Expression With the Tangent Trigonometric Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "026a56b8-1115-5684-ac6e-ef1630e64970" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+\\sqrt{x}{sec}^{2}⁡x}{2\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326960} {:answer/id #uuid "0393bd6c-c00c-5794-bb05-1c8299be09b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+2{sec}^{2}⁡\\sqrt{x}}{2\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326972} {:answer/id #uuid "25ff194a-cfcd-58a0-adba-8bc91fdf1efe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+2x{sec}^{2}⁡x}{2\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327262} {:answer/id #uuid "44d191be-38a4-500e-8cfb-76a9d83fe2e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+2\\sqrt{x}sec⁡x}{2\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327534} {:answer/id #uuid "9336f84c-bb60-54b1-ba79-495700d341f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+2\\sqrt{x}{sec}^{2}⁡x}{2\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9336f84c-bb60-54b1-ba79-495700d341f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+2\\sqrt{x}{sec}^{2}⁡x}{2\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328218} :answer-field/id #uuid "39c98579-7ff9-5677-8870-c03daabb35df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329297}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "db3973ed-197d-5377-b8db-60e027e16ad0" :question/math-academy-id "q-47983" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $y=\\sqrt{x}+tan⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(tan⁡x)={sec}^{2}⁡x.$ Applying this formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x}+tan⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}+{sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{1+2\\sqrt{x}{sec}^{2}⁡x}{2\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329846 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059675 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0de0627b-2568-5c02-8928-d14344df5a2f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of an Expression With Sine or Cosine Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58178d4f-4abd-58b7-80f3-235da8563710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2sin⁡x-2{x}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327707} {:answer/id #uuid "6e8ba3a3-8163-54a7-a89c-617f3758ea02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2sin⁡x-2{x}^{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327890} {:answer/id #uuid "abf48582-4f98-5a96-ba7c-6645540d8053" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2sin⁡x-2{x}^{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328424} {:answer/id #uuid "b8c3ad30-bf9c-5740-9f1f-85721ef68822" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2sin⁡x+2{x}^{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328543} {:answer/id #uuid "fb5d65e6-8cdc-5871-afa6-4d5b3c079a97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2sin⁡x-2{x}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329139}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abf48582-4f98-5a96-ba7c-6645540d8053" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2sin⁡x-2{x}^{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328424} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa72fd76-960d-5bcb-9cf5-7f47b8602241" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329616}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f32d0b6b-0338-505d-81ba-ba8c8d1669de" :question/math-academy-id "q-47985" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $y=-2cos⁡x+{x}^{-2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(cos⁡x)=-sin⁡x.$ Applying this formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2cos⁡x+{x}^{-2}) \\\\  & =-2(-sin⁡x)+(-2){x}^{-2-1} \\\\  & =2sin⁡x-2{x}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329847 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119978 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c0b7d95-c699-5762-aa44-449712f035c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of the Inverse of a Polynomial Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38ba450c-d188-5534-a6ad-9cafd867771b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2{({g}^{-1}(x))}^{2}}{{({g}^{-1}(x))}^{3}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327431} {:answer/id #uuid "5ac51d0f-a480-5257-9373-a102ae71dd07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{g}^{-1}(x)}{2{g}^{-1}(x)+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327728} {:answer/id #uuid "750ebad7-1376-571e-b002-d5de0ee49d71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2{g}^{-1}(x)}{{({g}^{-1}(x))}^{2}+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327940} {:answer/id #uuid "b6596677-9e79-5e4d-84e5-b8e191d29f04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{({g}^{-1}(x))}^{2}}{2{({g}^{-1}(x))}^{3}+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328519} {:answer/id #uuid "f6eb08b3-97ab-5448-916e-1bc51afd4120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{g}^{-1}(x)}{{({g}^{-1}(x))}^{3}+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329100}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6596677-9e79-5e4d-84e5-b8e191d29f04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{({g}^{-1}(x))}^{2}}{2{({g}^{-1}(x))}^{3}+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328519} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc99300b-987e-5768-8111-394ddb19ea49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329622}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "22ff07e4-057d-5a67-9809-969ec3b995f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-47990" :question/problem "If $g(x)=-{x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{x},$ find ${({g}^{-1})}^{′}(x)$ as a function of ${g}^{-1}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y=-{x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{x},$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=-{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{y}\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={g}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(-{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{y}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =-2y-\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =-\\frac{2{y}^{3}+2}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2{y}^{3}+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${g}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={g}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{g}^{-1}(x)] & =-\\frac{({g}^{-1}(x))^{2}}{2({g}^{-1}(x))^{3}+2} \\\\ {({g}^{-1})}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{({g}^{-1}(x))^{2}}{2({g}^{-1}(x))^{3}+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329849 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096621 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c547746-49c8-5a4a-85fe-c6b75493a354" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient Containing Other Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d04b0f4-3b5e-5a83-ab7c-fadd2fae0aa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{xcos⁡x+2sin⁡x}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327753} {:answer/id #uuid "79438a62-c77a-5744-9802-65b0e97ef42c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{cos⁡x-2xsin⁡x}{{x}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327973} {:answer/id #uuid "85f38040-e7a5-59fd-a359-c862f85f6a27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{xcos⁡x-2sin⁡x}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328088} {:answer/id #uuid "a8c4ce4c-f609-552a-9123-d8fd254ccab0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2xcos⁡x-xsin⁡x}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328396} {:answer/id #uuid "c0e8be1f-f487-5a62-b564-e74b12097ed9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{xsin⁡x-2cos⁡x}{{x}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328619}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85f38040-e7a5-59fd-a359-c862f85f6a27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{xcos⁡x-2sin⁡x}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328088} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f86cadc-4440-532f-bf1a-6161f5f6bfb9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329450}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b3c4b5b5-b682-57e6-baf5-0601260b1ed0" :question/math-academy-id "q-47998" :question/problem "If $f(x)=\\frac{sin⁡x}{{x}^{2}},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=sin⁡x$ and $v(x)={x}^{2}.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(sin⁡x)^{′}\\cdot {x}^{2}-sin⁡x\\cdot ({x}^{2})^{′}}{({x}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}cos⁡x-2xsin⁡x}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x(xcos⁡x-2sin⁡x)}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{xcos⁡x-2sin⁡x}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329850 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092260 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a83f86ab-a008-5487-b118-f1495a0fe4d6" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Linear Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "016b59fa-7dd1-5430-8a0d-9d17b256b87e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8{e}^{4x-1}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326950} {:answer/id #uuid "1a09b33b-87d5-53a9-afee-f8e02962d4c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{4x-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327160} {:answer/id #uuid "35f572ac-b945-560e-89f5-d45ed6858e1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{e}^{4x-1}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327403} {:answer/id #uuid "bab77687-86e3-5b97-960a-d832566921c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8{e}^{4x-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328563} {:answer/id #uuid "bfa829b5-29ee-5ba4-a1c8-72e5faa0588a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8{e}^{4x-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328611}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfa829b5-29ee-5ba4-a1c8-72e5faa0588a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8{e}^{4x-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328611} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2f4dd66-f6d3-5f50-8ce9-b004c416733f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329462}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dd0c521a-06b7-559f-8b78-1152ec8cbcd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-48007" :question/problem "Calculate ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)=-2{e}^{4x-1}+3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=-2{e}^{u}+3,\\,u=4x-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=-2{e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{u}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-8{e}^{u} \\\\  & =-8{e}^{4x-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392602 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068696 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31eacf5a-75a5-5b38-8c24-4b1598ecf3a7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Discontinuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d495799e-2f9d-5146-a399-506d9d32ad84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c9/c9ba1e63c665ad63597c822828fdf26ec0fefade083df5ea828f57a49d3aa149.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392604} {:answer/id #uuid "c796e42f-5eb7-5d3a-900e-6d5fb58916e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fc/fc1d428500558ed62fbf5f7e0c13604cbc759629cedb63a8f9c2f7fc0b412480.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392605} {:answer/id #uuid "742220de-47f9-5f30-8679-f64626ac489f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d9/d9185fb471c9806d490133c6ea5a54ef69878f89ca0e0be66008ec3626bfe346.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392606} {:answer/id #uuid "11ce7ab1-ca48-5c69-b7f7-b7ff62f3ea3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/49/49e7b3c3cd4e6410b95a307f74c90079ec61c2d3540f86a357db88c77039ca44.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392607} {:answer/id #uuid "c37ded1b-1a1e-52ed-a977-31d60b87248f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bd/bd662a57e07bf3f0319a00a15dc5c520a172d59672eae24f7999a4535da