er/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328364} {:answer/id #uuid "b013ab09-8aad-54e4-9a17-87cdc1aa5d04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328460} {:answer/id #uuid "c6636203-3372-5d9c-9639-986cdeaf7e30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ee867b9-645a-512d-9f99-f3aa8226ad71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327063} :answer-field/id #uuid "e45de7c6-c5ce-5b9d-a7b8-ea5254d09158" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329581}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "96069899-bca3-51de-b64c-6e29072ea13e" :question/math-academy-id "q-48062" :question/problem "![](images/d9/d9db7f2a491ae3065dbe8434a71d378c4fed8fec16c82e2fd76dcb209c786809.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)$ exists\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is false. From the graph we can see that no value has been assigned to $f(x)$ at $x=1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x) & =1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=1.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function is not even defined at $x=1.$ The graph contains a break at $x=1.$ It's impossible to draw the graph of $f(x)$ without taking our pencil off of the paper."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329855 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068697 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f92e521-e7ab-56ba-8eac-0484b140b1a2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Behavior of a Function in the Neighborhood of a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f05bd8f-eb2f-57d7-ae18-c11817bd78b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327620} {:answer/id #uuid "83335e31-f97c-5df4-863a-ef88150fb6f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328065} {:answer/id #uuid "9a9a91aa-3ed8-5151-bcb5-e59ccd13d6bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328272} {:answer/id #uuid "ce9e8871-1f30-5744-8374-042f56698b0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328750} {:answer/id #uuid "d6472410-aa22-5e41-a0c0-09e47c3fb93c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83335e31-f97c-5df4-863a-ef88150fb6f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328065} :answer-field/id #uuid "23e84077-b88d-5119-bcf7-6c45cbfd0278" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1e01edc7-98f4-52fe-bf43-cb1e05ae0d4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-48065" :question/problem "![](images/fa/fa15192923f9fd7370818fcb6b05b24b3bb6901dfbf90dd53539d71fd0f8b0bf.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=f(2)$\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$ and $f(2)=1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, from the graph we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x) & =1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=1=f(2).$\n\n- Statement III is true. The graph of $f(x)$ can be drawn without taking our pencil off of the paper in the neighborhood of $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329858 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092382 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "db808f48-6d0c-5b35-9342-b1817df65172" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Equation of a Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02cb37f5-2f1d-5582-9b0a-ba3d5ae84781" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326967} {:answer/id #uuid "12f35963-b5fb-5ee8-a217-7821558518c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327097} {:answer/id #uuid "29f970a0-530a-5098-9cef-2daea001ef9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327295} {:answer/id #uuid "39dad4a5-4110-5374-97bc-fe41c7c0f5d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5x-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327440} {:answer/id #uuid "83d0f077-ab57-54f6-80e1-8b63e8a4c95e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328072}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83d0f077-ab57-54f6-80e1-8b63e8a4c95e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328072} :answer-field/id #uuid "324d4519-c59d-5772-95b5-cc69664e41ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329278}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a5a7a5a2-50a1-5b97-8ac0-810e9082e127" :question/math-academy-id "q-4807" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=2{x}^{2}+x$ at the point $(1,3).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=2{x}^{2}+x.$ Computing the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2}+x)=4x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute ${f}^{′}(1)$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1)=4(1)+1=5\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=5$ and the coordinates of our point $(1,3),$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(3) & =5(x-1) \\\\ y & =5x-5+3 \\\\ y & =5x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069368 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068696 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31eacf5a-75a5-5b38-8c24-4b1598ecf3a7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Discontinuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9aa17146-c6c7-5f57-99cb-8b4f88241e1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 2$ and $x = 0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067379} {:answer/id #uuid "f56223ce-52c2-5805-b123-5b321403e168" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067380} {:answer/id #uuid "fe31fb35-056a-5723-b579-c5fcb9207404" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067381} {:answer/id #uuid "0f09c3a6-6f34-51bf-9fbc-ff068943f2ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 175